uckets  typically in any of these larger arcs  it's not too surprising that you can  think of them as as three acts and it's  a very traditional hollywood-esque  approach to storytelling the decision to  go with episodic was more driven out of  the experience we had working on  half-life 2 where we really weren't able  to give anything to our customers for  like a five-year period and that was  very hard on us as as game developers  because we thrive on the feedback we  received we we we get better when we  know what people think both positive and  negative and so with episodic it really  allowed us to tighten that loop up  enormous ly there are lots of benefits  in terms of managing scale in terms of  reacting to what's happening on the  hardware side but one of the biggest  reasons is simply so we don't get into a  situation where we have to wait five  years before hearing from the people who  play our games about what what works and  what we need to do better if you have a  limited amount of time that's the that's  the scarcest resource we have every  decision you make with your time is a  vote about what the company's priority  to be the question you should ask as a  programmer is what's the best way for me  to spend my time right now that creates  value for our customer there wasn't some  magic  pioneer fairy who hit us on the head and  allowed us to do pioneering work it's  really consequence of a bunch of other  decisions you make along the way how  your games are being funded how you  select your colleagues how you organize  your teams are really the things that  are going to allow you to do pioneering  work in our case we've designed the  company in such a way that we really not  only give everyone who works there the  opportunity but they all have the  expectation that they're going to be  doing original and creative work I am a  big believer in one person one office  DeMarcos people where if anybody's read  that you know I sort of treat that as a  Bible and so I was like I hated cubicles  like with a passion and a vengeance  it's like you know they grew out of this  data general philosophy of screw if  you're screwing your employees you're  probably doing something right and so  I'm like okay everybody always has their  own office and you know and you've got a  Do Not Disturb button on your phone and  you have a door you can close and you  can decorate your office every want and  people kept sneaking into other people's  offices and then they started tearing  doors down so everybody had their own  office but nobody was ever in it right  so they were all congregating in our  conference rooms and so we had all these  empty offices and all these clustered  clusters of people in conference rooms  and you know that's when I realized you  know they were making the right decision  right they were deciding that the the  there were benefits to being closer  proximity to other people and that  Gabe's rigid adherence to one person one  Hauk office was actually hurting the  their productivity so even you know hid  after being hit on the head long enough  I realized that everybody else was  making a decision that was correct for  them and so now we just say everybody  designs their own office space so our  largest single room right now is about  80 people in it I share an office with  two other people that each person you  know has to that's one of the things  that you think about you think about how  you communicate with other  people you think about what's an optimal  optimal work environment for the the  goals that you have desks are all on  wheels we only have two contact points  with the floor so you can pack up  enjoying a different group of people in  about 15 minutes some people are working  on the design of if anybody's done  paired programming there's some people  are trying to build a desk that's more  optimal for paired programming and  giving you a shared thing and they have  to make a decision whether it's more  worth their time to work on something  like that than it is to do the work that  they already are so we just sort of said  okay each person has to make their own  decisions about what they work on who  they're working with what are the tools  they need and what you know what what  their offices ought to look like but  that's that's how we evolve usually by  people deciding that I have a bad idea  and this one Pulaski  Oh  crankin you choose Turk the mass  all right three thank you so much for  watching I made this video because King  Naru Helio asked me to so there's a  famous entrepreneur that you want me to  profile next leave it in the comments  below and I'll see what I can do  I'd also love to know which of gabe  newell's top ten rules meant the most to  you had the biggest impact on your life  leave it in the comments and I'll join  the discussion thank you so much for  watching continue to believe and I'll  see you soon
 

